Review probes, round 3 (PR #314): reflection with a built-up name, and
computed keys in patterns. Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(Object.
getPrototypeOf(f), "constr" + "uctor").value is the Function constructor,
called directly or as a tagged template; { [k]: F } = f and
{ [`constr${"uctor"}`]: F } = f destructure it. The reflection APIs
(getOwnPropertyDescriptor(s), getPrototypeOf, setPrototypeOf,
getOwnPropertyNames, defineProperty/ies) are sensitive words, and every
computed property key that is not a number or a non-sensitive string is
unverified, in a pattern, an object literal or a class body alike (they
are not told apart). TypeScript index signatures and mapped types are
types, not keys, and stay verified; a key that starts like a mapped
type, [k in o ? a : b] (also ||, &&, ?? or a comma), selects a value
rather than a boolean and is unverified. Each reflection API is pinned
alone (define-properties, own-descriptors, set-prototype), and this before
a computed key is not the this.<name> form: this[k] reaching the Function
constructor, and this[i - 1], whose numeric key is otherwise safe. Controls already held: a computed
process.env key, import.meta.resolve then import(u), vm, and an escaped
eval.
